Extension methods are not a mock-oriented construct. They are static methods and, by
definition, it is not possible to mock a static construct in C#'s runtime. Thus, we need to
provide an abstraction over the ILogger factory that allows us to inject and mock the
interface that's used in our tests.
In general, it isn't possible to test static methods. Moreover, mock libraries
typically create mocks by dynamically creating classes at runtime.
Typically, they override the behavior of types by extending them. Since
the static methods cannot be overridden, it isn't possible to mock them.
Therefore, when we need to mock a static element, it is suggested that we
abstract them and encapsulate them into classes.
